insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 1, 'A loss of containment is handed to this engine. Which description matches what it returns?', '["How often the loss of containment occurs each year, read from failure data the engine carries for each kind of equipment.", "The physical effects together with the frequency of the release, so that the two can be multiplied inside the engine.", "Physical effects: how much gets out, where it goes, what a fire gives off, what a blast does and what fraction of the people exposed would be harmed.", "A consequence category for the scenario."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 1 says the engine turns a loss of containment into physical effects and computes effects and never frequencies. It carries no failure data, so it cannot say how often anything happens. It therefore has no frequency to multiply by an effect, and nothing in its exports assigns a category to a scenario; how often a release occurs belongs elsewhere.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 2, 'Every function in the engine ends one of two ways. What are they?', '["A result object carrying a `basis` block with the model, its source and its units, or an object with `error` and `field`.", "A result object with the offending input quietly replaced by a default, or a result object with a warning appended to it.", "A number with no basis, or an error string with no field named.", "A result object for a valid call, or a partly filled result object in which the fields computed before the offending input are kept and the rest are left empty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 1 states it: every function returns either a result object carrying a `basis` block (the model, its source and its units) or an object with `error` and `field`, where `field` names the offending input. The engine substitutes no default and fills in no partial result. A bare number without its basis never comes back, and every refusal names its field.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 3, 'An argument is named `windSpeed10mMS`. What does its name tell the analyst to type?', '["A wind speed in metres per second at the height of the release, whatever that height happens to be on the facility.", "A wind speed averaged over 10 minutes, in miles per second, since the MS suffix names a speed and the 10m part names a time.", "A mass rate in kilograms per second, since the suffix is read as the MS of a mass and the 10m part names a hole of 10 m.", "A wind speed in metres per second, taken at 10 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 2 tables the suffixes: MS is metres per second, and the 10m form is the wind at 10 m. It is not the wind at the release height. The 10m part names a height and has nothing to do with a time average, and a mass rate carries the suffix KgS, as in `massRateKgS`.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 4, 'Methane is typed as 0.01604 in one call and carbon monoxide as 28.01 in another. Why are both correct?', '["The engine detects a molar mass typed in g/mol and divides it by a thousand before it is used anywhere.", "The source terms take a molar mass in kg/mol and the concentration functions take one in g/mol, and the argument names say which.", "Both are in g/mol; methane is simply a much lighter molecule than carbon monoxide, which the engine takes on trust.", "Both are in kg/mol, and the engine carries two different ideal gas constants, one for the source terms and one for the concentrations, so either scale works."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 2 says two molar mass units coexist: the source terms take kg/mol (methane is 0.01604, stated) and the concentration functions take g/mol (carbon monoxide is 28.01, stated), and `molarMassKgMol` and `molarMassGMol` say which. The engine never converts a unit it was not asked to, so it does not rescale a figure. Methane at 0.01604 g/mol would be absurd, and the engine exports one gas constant, `R_J_MOL_K`, 8.314462618.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 5, 'A vessel gauge reads its pressure above the atmosphere, and the analyst types that gauge figure into `upstreamPressurePa`. What has gone wrong?', '["Nothing, because the engine adds `ATM_PA` to any pressure it is given before comparing it with ambient.", "The engine reads every pressure as absolute, so the driving pressure is understated by one atmosphere, 101325 Pa.", "The engine refuses the call outright, because a gauge figure would carry the suffix Pag and this argument name ends in Pa.", "The driving pressure is overstated by one atmosphere, 101325 Pa, so the mass rate the engine returns comes out too high."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 2: pressures are absolute, ambient is `ATM_PA`, 101325 Pa, and a gauge pressure typed where an absolute one is due understates the driving pressure by one atmosphere. The engine never converts a unit it was not asked to, so it adds nothing. No suffix separates gauge from absolute, so there is no refusal. The error runs low: typing the smaller gauge figure understates the pressure.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 6, 'A liquid outflow call is given a discharge coefficient above one. What comes back?', '["A mass rate computed with the coefficient clipped to one, and a warning is appended to the result saying that the Yellow Book recommends 0.62 for a sharp orifice.", "A mass rate computed at the Yellow Book value of 0.62 in place of the typed figure.", "A mass rate computed at the typed value, since the coefficient is a stated input and the choice of it belongs to the analyst, whatever figure is typed.", "A refusal naming `dischargeCoefficient`, in the engine''s words: \"dischargeCoefficient: must lie in (0, 1]: the YB recommends 0.62 for a sharp orifice\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 3 tables this refusal: the field named is `dischargeCoefficient`, and that string is the engine''s own. A refusal carries no number, so there is no clipped rate, no substituted 0.62 and no rate at the typed value. The coefficient must lie in (0, 1], and the message names the Yellow Book''s 0.62 as its recommendation without applying it.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 7, 'A tank has its ullage below ambient and no liquid above the hole. Which field does the engine name, and in what words?', '["`pressureAboveLiquidPa`: \"pressureAboveLiquidPa: the pressure at the hole does not exceed ambient, so nothing flows out\"", "`liquidHeadM`: a liquid height of 0 m or more above the hole is required before anything can be computed", "`upstreamPressurePa`: must exceed the ambient pressure, or nothing flows out", "`holeDiameterM`: no hole is open"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 3 gives the row for an ullage below ambient and no head: the field is `pressureAboveLiquidPa`, and the quoted string is the engine''s message. A head of zero is itself allowed (the head must be 0 m or more), so `liquidHeadM` is not the field named. The `upstreamPressurePa` message belongs to the gas outflow function, and the hole is not what is wrong.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 8, 'A spill is passed to `poolFromSpill` with no bund area and no thickness. Why does the engine refuse it?', '["It spreads the spill until the depth falls to a default of one millimetre, and refuses only when the area exceeds a limit.", "It needs the spill volume in litres, and a volume in m3 with nothing else is read as an empty call.", "It has no spreading model, so a pool needs either a bund floor or a thickness the analyst states.", "It has a spreading model but needs the ground type."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s words for this call are "bundAreaM2: a bund area (confined pool) or a pool thickness (unconfined pool) is required: no spreading model is implemented". There is no default thickness and no spreading of any kind, so there is no area limit to reach. The volume is `spillVolumeM3`, in m3 as its name says, and no ground type is asked for because nothing spreads.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 9, 'A plume is asked for at a wind speed of zero. What does the engine say?', '["It returns the concentration of the class F plume at the lowest wind the Briggs table carries, and warns that the air was calm.", "It returns an infinite concentration at every receptor, since the plume expression divides by a wind speed of zero.", "It returns a concentration from its calm-air form of the plume, with a warning that the form is an extrapolation.", "\"windSpeedMS: must be above 0 m/s: the Gaussian plume divides by the wind speed and has no calm-air form\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 3 quotes the engine''s own message for this call, naming `windSpeedMS`. A refusal carries no number, so there is neither an infinite concentration nor a substituted class F result, and the Briggs table holds sigma coefficients and no winds. The plume has no calm-air form at all, which is exactly what the message says.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 10, '`briggsRuralSigmas` is asked for class G. What comes back, and which classes does it name?', '["A refusal on `stabilityClass` whose message says the class must be a Pasquill-Gifford class, one of A, B, C, D, E, F.", "Class F sigmas with a warning, since G is read as the next class beyond the most stable one in the table.", "Class D sigmas, the neutral class, returned by default for any letter the table does not carry, with a warning in the basis block.", "A refusal on `stabilityClass` naming A to D."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 3 gives the message "stabilityClass: must be a Pasquill-Gifford class, one of A, B, C, D, E, F", and `STABILITY_CLASSES` in section 1 exports exactly those six. The engine does not fall back on class F or on the neutral class D, because a refusal carries no number. E and F are in the table, so a list stopping at D is wrong.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 11, 'Which of the engine''s six sources fixes the Briggs rural sigma coefficients and the continuous Gaussian plume with ground reflection?', '["The TNO Yellow Book CPR 14E (2005), which also fixes liquid and gas outflow through a hole.", "The TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999), which also supplies a worked carbon monoxide plume.", "ALOHA, the NOAA TM NOS OR&R 43 technical documentation (2013).", "CCOHS, the source of the molar volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 4 says ALOHA fixes the continuous Gaussian plume with ground reflection and the Briggs rural sigma coefficients. The Yellow Book fixes the outflow, the pool diameter and the evaporation. The Purple Book supplies a worked carbon monoxide plume, which serves as a published check on the plume; CCOHS fixes only ppm and mg/m3 through a molar volume.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 12, 'The engine names CCOHS as the source for one conversion. What does that source fix?', '["The molar mass of every substance the plume can carry, from a table the engine exports beside `STABILITY_CLASSES`.", "ppm and mg/m3 through a molar volume, printed there as 24.45 L/mol at 25 C and 760 torr.", "The Briggs rural coefficients for sigma_y and sigma_z in open country.", "The ideal gas constant 8.314462618, which the engine defines itself."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 4 prints the CCOHS string, "Converting occupational exposure limits from mg/m3 to ppm (24.45 L/mol at 25 C, 760 torr)", and says it fixes ppm and mg/m3 through a molar volume. The engine carries no table of molar masses: the molar mass is a stated input. The Briggs coefficients come from ALOHA, and `R_J_MOL_K` is an engine constant that no source row claims.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 13, 'The Gaussian plume at 500 m in class D reports a source string in its basis. What is the point of that string?', '["The result carries its own citation: the model''s source and, here, that the sigmas are Briggs rural from ALOHA Table 13.", "It is a comment for the developers only, since a learner never sees the basis block of any result.", "It shows the result was checked against a published value at that very distance and class before it was returned.", "It lists every source the engine knows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 4 says every basis block names the source it used, so a result carries its own citation, and quotes this one: "NOAA TM NOS OR&R 43, ALOHA Technical Documentation (2013) section 4.3; sigmas Briggs rural (ALOHA Table 13)". The basis is part of the returned result and is what a note quotes. It names the source of the method and says nothing of a check at 500 m. It names the one source used, and the full list is `CONSEQUENCE_SOURCES`.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 14, 'One word in this course is always qualified and never written bare, and it is qualified four ways. Which list gives the four qualified names?', '["Heat flux, burning rate, evaporation rate and mass rate, each with its own unit in the argument name.", "Burning flux, evaporation flux, mass flux and the release rate, the last being the kilograms per second leaving the hole.", "Heat flux, burning flux, surface emissive power and view factor, all of them per square metre of flame.", "Heat flux, burning flux, evaporation flux and mass flux."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 34 names them: a heat flux in W/m2, a burning flux in kg/(m2 s), an evaporation flux and a mass flux all appear in this one course, and each is always qualified. A mass rate or an evaporation rate is kilograms per second and is not a quantity per square metre. The release through a hole is a mass rate, `massRateKgS`. A view factor is a fraction with no unit.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-consequence-model-computes', 15, 'The digest tables how many refusals, across how many functions?', '["24 refusals across 37 functions, one refusal for every function the engine exports and more functions than refusals.", "6 refusals, one for each of the Pasquill-Gifford classes, across the dispersion section alone.", "37 refusals across 24 functions.", "37 refusals, all of them in the source terms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 3 closes its table with "37 refusals are tabled above, across 24 functions". Reversing the two counts is wrong, and the refusals span every one of the engine''s five sections. The six Pasquill-Gifford classes are what one refusal message lists and are no count of refusals.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 1, 'In the engine''s liquid outflow model, P = rhoL g hL + PaL. What is P?', '["The pressure above the liquid alone, typed as a gauge figure, since the head is added later inside the square root.", "The absolute pressure at the hole: the static head of the liquid plus the pressure above the liquid.", "The driving pressure, already net of ambient, which the engine multiplies by the density and takes the square root of.", "The ambient pressure outside the hole."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 5 says P is the absolute pressure at the hole, the static head rhoL g hL plus the pressure above the liquid, PaL, and the mass rate depends on P minus ambient. P is not the driving pressure itself, because ambient has not yet been taken off it. Every pressure is absolute, so PaL is never a gauge figure, and ambient is the Pa that P is compared with.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 2, 'On the AMENAM line the pressure at the hole is 250013.915000 Pa. Which pressure sets the mass rate of 19.354651 kg/s?', '["The pressure at the hole, 250013.915000 Pa, since the model string carries the absolute P under its square root.", "The pressure above the liquid, 200000 Pa absolute, as typed for the line.", "The static head on its own, 98675.000000 Pa, since the ullage pressure only matters once the head is gone.", "The driving pressure, 148688.915000 Pa."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The model is qS = Cd Ah sqrt(2 (P - Pa) rhoL), so the square root holds P minus ambient, the driving pressure, which section 5 prints as 148688.915000 Pa. The 250013.915000 Pa is P before ambient is taken off. 200000 Pa is the stated ullage. 98675.000000 Pa is the driving pressure the head sweep prints at a head of 0 m, which is the ullage over ambient with no liquid above the hole.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 3, 'The AMENAM line is modelled with the ullage left at ambient, 101325 Pa, in place of its 200000 Pa blanket. What mass rate does the engine return?', '["11.225132 kg/s, from the head alone.", "15.768131 kg/s, the 150000 Pa row.", "19.354651 kg/s, since the head of 6 m is unchanged and the head alone sets the rate.", "15.767020 kg/s, the 0 m head row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ullage sweep prints 11.225132 kg/s at 101325 Pa, with a driving pressure of 50013.915000 Pa: with the ullage at ambient only the head drives the flow. 15.768131 kg/s belongs to the 150000 Pa row. 19.354651 kg/s needs the 200000 Pa blanket. 15.767020 kg/s is the head sweep''s row at 0 m, where the blanket drives the flow with no liquid above the hole.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 4, 'With the ullage at ambient the jet velocity from the AMENAM hole is Torricelli''s sqrt(2 g h). What does the engine return?', '["18.704445 m/s, the jet velocity with the 200000 Pa blanket still in place above the liquid.", "11.225132 m/s, since the jet velocity and the mass rate carry the same figure at ambient ullage.", "10.848032 m/s, matching the derived Torricelli figure.", "9.80665 m/s, the gravitational acceleration."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 5 derives 10.848032 m/s from sqrt(2 g h) and the engine gives 10.848032. 18.704445 m/s is the jet velocity at the stated 200000 Pa ullage. 11.225132 is the mass rate in kg/s at ambient ullage, a different quantity in a different unit. 9.80665 is `G_M_S2`, an acceleration in m/s2 and never a velocity.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 5, 'The AMENAM level falls until no liquid stands above the hole, with the 200000 Pa ullage held. Does anything still flow?', '["No, because a head of 0 m is refused with the message that nothing flows out once the liquid height reaches zero.", "No: with the head at 0 m the driving pressure is zero whatever the ullage, and the engine returns 0 kg/s.", "Yes: the ullage over ambient drives 98675.000000 Pa and the engine returns 15.767020 kg/s.", "Yes, 11.225132 kg/s, the head alone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The head sweep''s 0 m row prints a driving pressure of 98675.000000 Pa and 15.767020 kg/s: the blanket alone pushes the liquid out. A head of 0 m is allowed (the refusal is for a NEGATIVE head, and the ullage refusal needs the pressure at the hole not to exceed ambient). The driving pressure is not zero because the ullage is above ambient. 11.225132 kg/s is the ambient ullage row with the full 6 m head.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 6, 'The AMENAM head is doubled from 6 m to 12 m with the ullage held. Why does the mass rate go from 19.354651 to only 22.374228 kg/s?', '["The rate goes as the square root of the driving pressure, and the driving pressure includes the ullage, so doubling the head raises it from 148688.915000 to 198702.830000 Pa.", "The rate is linear in the head, but the discharge coefficient falls as the head rises, which offsets part of the gain.", "The engine caps the head at the height of the tank wall.", "The jet velocity is fixed by the hole, so only the density term grows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The model carries sqrt(2 (P - Pa) rhoL), and the head sweep shows the driving pressure going from 148688.915000 Pa at 6 m to 198702.830000 Pa at 12 m, because the ullage contribution does not double with the head. The discharge coefficient is a stated input that never changes with the head. The liquid model has no tank wall, and the jet velocity is not fixed by the hole; the density is a stated input.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 7, 'Everything on the AMENAM line is held and the discharge coefficient is raised from 0.62 to 1. What mass rate comes back?', '["24.973743 kg/s, which the coefficient sweep prints at 0.8.", "19.354651 kg/s, since the Yellow Book value of 0.62 is always used.", "18.730307 kg/s, which the sweep prints at 0.6.", "31.217178 kg/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The coefficient sweep prints 31.217178 kg/s at 1, and the mass rate is linear in the discharge coefficient. 24.973743 and 18.730307 kg/s are the rows at 0.8 and 0.6. The engine uses the typed coefficient; 0.62 is only what its refusal message recommends for a sharp orifice, and 1 lies inside (0, 1] so it is accepted.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 8, 'The AMENAM hole is doubled from 0.05 m to 0.1 m in diameter. By what factor does the mass rate change, and why?', '["By 2, because the mass rate is linear in the hole diameter, just as it is linear in the discharge coefficient.", "By 4.000000, because the rate is linear in the hole area and the area goes as the square of the diameter.", "By 1.414214, since the diameter enters the model under the square root with the pressure.", "By 4.000000, because the jet velocity doubles as well as the diameter."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 5 says the mass rate is linear in the discharge coefficient and in the hole AREA, so doubling the diameter multiplies it by 4.000000, from 19.354651 to 77.418603 kg/s. It is not linear in the diameter. The square root holds the driving pressure and the density; 1.414214 is the pool diameter factor for a halved thickness in section 8. The jet velocity depends on the driving pressure alone and does not change with the hole.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 9, 'The engine prints hole areas to twelve decimals. What area does it report for the 0.025 m hole on the AMENAM line?', '["0.001963495408 m2, the area it reports for the line''s own 0.05 m hole.", "0.000078539816 m2, the area of the 0.01 m hole in the same sweep.", "0.007853981634 m2, the area of the 0.1 m hole in the same sweep.", "0.000490873852 m2."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The hole sweep prints 0.000490873852 m2 for 0.025 m, with a mass rate of 4.838663 kg/s. 0.001963495408 m2 belongs to 0.05 m, 0.000078539816 m2 to 0.01 m and 0.007853981634 m2 to 0.1 m; each is pi d squared over four for its own diameter, so none of them is the 0.025 m hole.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 10, 'The AMENAM ullage is doubled from 500000 to 1000000 Pa with the head held. What does the engine return, and why does the rate not double?', '["19.354651 kg/s, since the head sets the rate and the ullage only moves the pressure at the hole.", "48.888640 kg/s against 33.621626 kg/s, because the rate goes as the square root of the driving pressure.", "33.621626 kg/s, since a liquid outflow chokes and further pressure adds nothing.", "48.888640 kg/s, because the head term falls as the ullage rises."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ullage sweep prints 33.621626 kg/s at 500000 Pa and 48.888640 kg/s at 1000000 Pa; the driving pressure goes from 448688.915000 to 948688.915000 Pa and the rate follows its square root. 19.354651 kg/s is the rate at the stated 200000 Pa ullage, and the ullage plainly moves the rate across the sweep. Linear growth in pressure is the choked GAS result of section 6, and a liquid through a hole is never choked in this model. The head term rhoL g hL does not depend on the ullage at all.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 11, 'The Yellow Book acrylonitrile case prints 58.44 kg/s and the engine gives 58.429567 kg/s. Why does the golden allow 0.0005 relative?', '["The level is printed to two decimals, 11.12 m, so the printed rate cannot be matched more closely than that rounding allows.", "The engine uses a discharge coefficient of 0.6 where the book uses 0.62, and the tolerance absorbs the difference.", "The book computes the rate at time zero and the engine at 500 s.", "The relative difference is 1.79e-4 because the engine rounds the density of 812.5 kg/m3 before use."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 5: the engine gives 58.429567 kg/s against the printed 58.44, a relative difference of 1.79e-4, and the level is printed to two decimals, which is why the golden allows 0.0005 relative. Both use Cd 0.62, and both are at 500 s. The density is the stated 812.5 kg/m3 and nothing is rounded by the engine.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 12, 'The same Yellow Book example prints 60.915 kg/s at time zero for a level of 11.2 m. What does the golden do with that figure?', '["It gates the engine against 60.915 kg/s at a looser tolerance, because the value at time zero is the one the book prints first.", "It records a heat capacity ratio as inferred so that the engine reproduces 60.915 kg/s at time zero.", "It sets it aside: Bernoulli at that level gives 58.639369 kg/s, so the golden uses the value at 500 s, which reproduces.", "It uses 60.915 as the engine''s answer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 5 calls this an erratum in the published example: Bernoulli at 11.2 m gives 58.639369 kg/s, so the value at 500 s reproduces and the value at time zero does not, and the golden uses the one that reproduces. It does not gate against a figure that does not reproduce. A heat capacity ratio belongs to the gas outflow of section 6 and plays no part in a liquid. The erratum is a fact about the published source and changes nothing the engine returns.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 13, 'A liquid outflow call is given a head of minus 1 m. What does the engine return?', '["\"liquidHeadM: must be a liquid height of 0 m or more above the hole\"", "A mass rate computed as if the head were 0 m.", "\"pressureAboveLiquidPa: the pressure at the hole does not exceed ambient, so nothing flows out\"", "A mass rate with the head taken as 1 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 3 gives this row: a negative liquid head is refused on `liquidHeadM`, and that string is the engine''s own. A refusal carries no number, so nothing is computed at 0 m or at 1 m. The `pressureAboveLiquidPa` message is the refusal for an ullage below ambient with no head, a different row.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 14, 'A liquid outflow call is given a hole diameter of zero. Which field does the refusal name, and what does it offer instead?', '["`holeAreaM2`, and its message says the analyst must compute the area first.", "`dischargeCoefficient`, since a zero hole is read as a coefficient out of range.", "`massRateKgS`, and its message says the mass rate would be zero.", "`holeDiameterM`, and its message says a hole diameter above 0 m (or holeAreaM2) is required."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 3: "holeDiameterM: a hole diameter above 0 m (or holeAreaM2) is required". The field named is `holeDiameterM`, and the message offers the hole area as an alternative input without requiring it. The discharge coefficient has its own refusal, and `massRateKgS` is a result, which no refusal names.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-liquid-through-a-hole', 15, 'What single condition decides whether the liquid model has anything to compute?', '["The ullage must exceed ambient, since the head alone never drives a liquid out in this model.", "The absolute pressure at the hole must exceed ambient, whether from the head, the ullage or both.", "The head must exceed 0 m.", "The upstream pressure over ambient must reach the critical pressure ratio before any liquid leaves the hole."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mass rate depends on P minus ambient, and the engine refuses with "pressureAboveLiquidPa: the pressure at the hole does not exceed ambient, so nothing flows out" when that difference is not positive. The head alone drives 11.225132 kg/s on the AMENAM line at ambient ullage, and the ullage alone drives 15.767020 kg/s at a head of 0 m, so neither is needed on its own. The critical pressure ratio belongs to gas outflow.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 1, 'The AMENAM GAS methane line is set to 180000 Pa absolute. Ambient over upstream is 0.562917 and the critical pressure ratio is 0.543927. What regime does the engine report?', '["CHOKED, because the upstream pressure is well above ambient, with psi of 1.000000 and 0.129090 kg/s.", "CHOKED, since any ratio below one chokes the hole, with 0.092868 kg/s.", "SUBSONIC, with psi of 0.958958 and 0.074275 kg/s, since the flow is below the critical ratio.", "SUBSONIC, with an outflow coefficient psi of 0.999177 and 0.092868 kg/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flow is CHOKED only when ambient over upstream is at or below the critical pressure ratio. 0.562917 lies above 0.543927, so the 180000 Pa row is SUBSONIC with psi 0.999177 and 0.092868 kg/s. 0.129090 kg/s with psi 1.000000 is the 250000 Pa row, the first choked one. A ratio below one only means gas flows. Psi 0.958958 and 0.074275 kg/s belong to 150000 Pa.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 2, 'Once the AMENAM GAS line is choked, the upstream pressure is raised from 250000 to 10000000 Pa. How does the mass rate respond?', '["It grows by the square root of 40.000000, because the rate goes as the square root of the driving pressure as it does for a liquid.", "It grows by 40.000000, the same as the pressure ratio, from 0.129090 to 5.163609 kg/s.", "It stays at 0.129090 kg/s, since a choked hole passes a fixed mass rate whatever the upstream pressure.", "It grows by 40.000000 squared, as density and speed both rise."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 6: once choked the mass rate is LINEAR in the upstream pressure, and from 250000 to 10000000 Pa it grows by 40.000000, the same as the pressure ratio. The square root law is the liquid model''s. The rate does not stay fixed: the table prints 0.129090 kg/s at 250000 Pa and 5.163609 kg/s at 10000000 Pa. The upstream density grows by the same 40.000000, from 1.607640 to 64.305619 kg/m3, while the choked speed is set by the temperature and the gas, so nothing squares the ratio.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 3, 'What is the outflow coefficient psi on the AMENAM GAS line, and what does it do at the choked rows?', '["The Yellow Book subsonic outflow coefficient; it sits below one on the subsonic rows (0.756455 at 120000 Pa) and equals 1.000000 on every choked row.", "The discharge coefficient of the hole, 0.62 on every row, which the engine multiplies into the mass rate.", "The critical pressure ratio for the gas, 0.543927, repeated on each row for reference.", "A factor that rises above one once choked, 1.607640 at 250000 Pa, which is why the rate grows linearly."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The model string says "subsonic psi from YB 2.25", and the table shows psi 0.756455, 0.958958 and 0.999177 on the subsonic rows and 1.000000 on every choked row. The discharge coefficient is a separate stated input, 0.62. The critical pressure ratio is a threshold on ambient over upstream. 1.607640 is the upstream density in kg/m3 at 250000 Pa, and psi never exceeds one.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 4, 'For which heat capacity ratio does the engine''s table print a critical pressure ratio of 0.486669, and what upstream pressure chokes that gas into 101325 Pa?', '["1.1, choking at 173300.135461 Pa, since a smaller ratio gives a smaller critical pressure ratio.", "1.4, choking at 191801.047009 Pa.", "1.67, choking at 208201.258009 Pa.", "1.31, choking at 186284.176006 Pa, the methane line."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The table against the heat capacity ratio prints 0.486669 for 1.67 and a choking pressure of 208201.258009 Pa. 1.1 gives 0.584679 and 173300.135461 Pa: the critical pressure ratio FALLS as the heat capacity ratio rises, which is the reverse of what the first distractor claims. 1.4 gives 0.528282 and 191801.047009 Pa. 1.31 gives 0.543927 and 186284.176006 Pa.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 5, 'Two gases share the AMENAM hole and temperature; one has a heat capacity ratio of 1.1 and the other 1.67. Which needs the higher upstream pressure to choke into the atmosphere?', '["The 1.67 gas, because its critical pressure ratio is lower, 0.486669 against 0.584679.", "The 1.1 gas, because a gas closer to one has the higher critical pressure ratio and so needs more pressure to choke.", "Neither: every gas chokes at twice atmospheric pressure.", "The 1.1 gas, 173300.135461 Pa against 208201.258009 Pa."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 6 says the critical pressure ratio falls as the heat capacity ratio rises, so a gas with a larger ratio needs a higher upstream pressure to choke: 208201.258009 Pa for 1.67 against 173300.135461 Pa for 1.1. A higher critical pressure ratio is reached at a LOWER upstream pressure, so the 1.1 gas chokes sooner. The table shows no single choking pressure shared by every gas.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 6, 'The Yellow Book hydrogen example (50 bar, 288.15 K, d 0.1 m, Cd 0.62) prints 15.31 kg/s and does not print its heat capacity ratio. What does the golden record?', '["A ratio of 1.4, the usual value for a diatomic gas, accepting 15.292930 kg/s as close enough to the printed figure.", "A ratio of 1.31, the methane line''s, since the example leaves the ratio to the analyst.", "A ratio of 1.405 as printed in the example.", "A ratio of 1.405, marked INFERRED, because it reproduces 15.31 to two decimals and 1.4 gives 15.292930."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 6: gamma is NOT printed, and 1.405 is inferred because it gives 15.311760 kg/s, reproducing the printed 15.31 to its two decimals, while 1.4 gives 15.292930 and does not. The golden records the ratio as INFERRED and never as printed. 1.31 belongs to the methane teaching line and to no hydrogen source.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 7, 'For six gas cases the oracle takes a second route to the mass rate. What is it, and what does it show?', '["It repeats the engine''s ratio test in Python, so the two agree exactly by construction and the agreement shows the code was copied correctly.", "It reads the six rates off the Yellow Book tables, and the engine matches them to the printed decimals.", "It maximises the isentropic nozzle mass flux over every throat pressure at or above ambient, deriving choking instead of testing a ratio, and agrees with the engine to within 5.80e-16 relative.", "It runs the engine twice with different compilers."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 6 describes route B: the oracle maximises the isentropic nozzle mass flux over every throat pressure at or above ambient, which derives choking instead of testing a ratio, and the relative differences run from 2.15e-16 to 5.80e-16. Restating the same test would catch nothing, which is why the route is independent. Only the hydrogen case is a published Yellow Book figure, and nothing is run twice.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 8, 'Where the ratio of ambient over upstream lands exactly on the critical pressure ratio, what regime does the engine report?', '["SUBSONIC, because choking needs the ratio strictly below the critical value.", "CHOKED, because the Yellow Book tests at or above the critical value and the engine counts the boundary as choked.", "A refusal, because the regime is undefined at the boundary.", "CHOKED or SUBSONIC at random, depending on floating point rounding in the last digit of the ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s model string says "exactly at the ratio counts as choked", and section 7 says the Yellow Book tests P0 over Pa AT OR ABOVE ((gamma + 1) / 2) to the power gamma / (gamma - 1). The boundary is a defined choice, so there is no refusal and nothing random, and a tie is choked.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 9, 'On the AMENAM GAS line at 186284.176006 Pa the engine reports CHOKED; one part in a billion lower it reports SUBSONIC. How far apart are the two mass rates?', '["About 1 percent, the step between the subsonic and choked branches that the Yellow Book warns of.", "1.00e-9 relative: psi is 1.000000000000 on both sides, so the label flips with no jump in the rate.", "About 0.543927 relative, the size of the critical pressure ratio of the methane line itself.", "A step of its own, since just below the boundary psi takes the 0.999177 printed for the last subsonic row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 7 prints it: psi is 1.000000000000 on both sides and the two mass rates differ by, derived, 1.00e-9 relative. The subsonic outflow coefficient equals one there, so the flag flips with no jump, and the regime is a label on a continuous curve. There is no percentage step, and 0.543927 is a threshold on the pressure ratio that says nothing about a gap between rates. 0.999177 is psi at 180000 Pa, well above the boundary; one part in a billion below it psi still prints 1.000000000000.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 10, 'The golden case air-barely-subsonic has ambient over upstream 0.53328947368421054 against a critical pressure ratio of 0.52828178771717416. What does the engine return?', '["CHOKED, with psi 1.000000000000 and 0.113903 kg/s, since the two ratios agree to two decimals.", "SUBSONIC, with psi 0.756455 and 0.046872 kg/s, the lowest subsonic row of the methane line.", "CHOKED, since the ratio 0.53328947368421054 sits below the critical ratio of the methane line, 0.543927.", "SUBSONIC, with psi 0.999944960481 and 0.112711 kg/s."]'::jsonb, 3, '0.53328947368421054 is above 0.52828178771717416, so the case is SUBSONIC with psi 0.999944960481, just below one, and 0.112711 kg/s. CHOKED with 0.113903 kg/s is air-barely-choked, at 0.52773437499999998. Psi 0.756455 is the methane line at 120000 Pa. The test uses the gas''s own critical ratio, 0.52828178771717416 for air; the methane line''s 0.543927 plays no part.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 11, '`gasOrificeDischarge` is given an upstream pressure equal to ambient. What does the engine return?', '["\"upstreamPressurePa: must exceed the ambient pressure, or nothing flows out\"", "A SUBSONIC result at 0 kg/s with psi of zero.", "\"pressureAboveLiquidPa: the pressure at the hole does not exceed ambient, so nothing flows out\"", "A CHOKED result, since a ratio of one is at or below the critical ratio."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 3 tables this refusal on `upstreamPressurePa`, and the quoted string is the engine''s own. A refusal carries no number, so there is no zero rate. The `pressureAboveLiquidPa` message belongs to the liquid function. A ratio of one lies ABOVE every critical ratio in the table, so it could never be choked.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 12, 'A call types the heat capacity ratio as 1. Which message comes back?', '["A CHOKED result, since at a ratio of one the critical pressure ratio falls to zero and every flow chokes.", "A result computed with the ratio of 1.4, the default the engine carries for air and other diatomic gases.", "\"heatCapacityRatio: gamma = Cp/Cv must be above 1\"", "\"upstreamTemperatureK: must be an absolute temperature above 0 K\""]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 3: a heat capacity ratio of one is refused on `heatCapacityRatio` in those words, because the critical pressure ratio expression divides by gamma minus one. The engine carries no default ratio: 1.4 is a stated input in the table. The temperature message is a different refusal, for zero kelvin.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 13, 'An analyst types the upstream temperature as 0, meaning degrees Celsius. What happens?', '["The engine converts 0 C to 273.15 K and computes the rate, since the argument is a temperature.", "The engine computes an infinite upstream density and reports the mass rate as CHOKED at no limit, with a warning attached.", "The engine takes the 300 K of the methane line as a default and returns 0.129090 kg/s at 250000 Pa.", "It is refused: \"upstreamTemperatureK: must be an absolute temperature above 0 K\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The name `upstreamTemperatureK` says kelvin, the engine never converts a unit it was not asked to, and zero kelvin is refused in the quoted words. A refusal carries no number, so there is no infinite density and no result. 300 K is a stated input of the teaching line, and the engine carries no default temperature.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 14, 'Why does lowering the ambient pressure below a choked hole leave its mass rate unchanged?', '["At or below the critical pressure ratio the downstream pressure cannot reach back into the hole.", "Because the engine fixes ambient at 101325 Pa and never reads a different ambient.", "It does change: the rate rises as the ambient pressure falls, whatever the regime of the flow.", "Because a choked hole passes the same mass rate for every upstream pressure too."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 6: at or below the critical pressure ratio the downstream pressure cannot reach back into the hole, which is what choked means. Ambient defaults to `ATM_PA` unless a call states otherwise, so it can be changed. A choked rate still grows linearly with the UPSTREAM pressure, by 40.000000 from 250000 to 10000000 Pa.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-gas-through-a-hole', 15, 'In the gas model string, the critical test reads Pa/P0 <= (2/(gamma+1))^(gamma/(gamma-1)). Which pressures are Pa and P0?', '["Pa is the pressure in pascals of the hole and P0 is ambient at sea level, 101325 Pa.", "Pa is ambient and P0 is the upstream pressure, both absolute.", "Pa is the upstream gauge pressure and P0 is ambient.", "Pa is the driving pressure and P0 is the pressure at the hole, as in the liquid model string."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 6 tabulates ambient over upstream as "Pa over P0" and says the flow is CHOKED when ambient over upstream is at or below the critical pressure ratio; pressures are absolute throughout (section 2). Ambient is Pa, and no gauge figure appears. The liquid model''s driving pressure and pressure at the hole are a different model.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 1, 'A spill of 30 m3 lands in a bund of 400 m2 with a 0.5 m wall. What pool does the engine return?', '["UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS, 3000.000000 m2 at a thickness of 0.01 m, equivalent diameter 61.803872 m, because the spill spreads past the wall.", "CONFINED, 400.000000 m2, 0.5 m deep, the height of the wall.", "CONFINED, 400.000000 m2, 0.075000 m deep, equivalent diameter 22.567583 m.", "CONFINED, 600.000000 m2 at 0.05 m, diameter 27.639532 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 8: the pool covers the bund floor, 400 m2, at a depth of 0.075000 m (30 m3 over 400 m2) and an equivalent diameter of 22.567583 m from D = sqrt(4 A / pi). The spill stands well below the 0.5 m wall, so it is confined and nothing spreads. The wall height only decides overtopping and is never the depth. 600.000000 m2 and 27.639532 m are the unconfined row at 0.05 m.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 2, 'Shrink the bund floor to 50 m2 and keep its wall. What does the engine do with the same spill?', '["It refuses: the spill would stand 0.600000 m deep, above the wall, and the message is \"spillVolumeM3: the spill overtops the bund (depth above the wall height), so the pool is not confined by it\"", "It returns a CONFINED pool 0.600000 m deep over 50 m2, with a warning that the depth is above the wall.", "It returns a CONFINED pool 0.5 m deep over 50 m2 and treats the rest of the spill as lost over the wall.", "It spreads the overflow outside the bund and returns a larger UNCONFINED pool around it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 8: in a 50 m2 bund the spill would stand, derived, 0.600000 m deep behind a 0.5 m wall, and the engine refuses on `spillVolumeM3` in the quoted words. A refusal carries no number, so there is no warned result and no truncated pool. The engine has no spreading model, so it cannot spread the overflow either.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 3, 'No bund applies and the analyst states a thickness of 0.01 m for the 30 m3 spill. What does the engine return?', '["UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS, 1500.000000 m2, equivalent diameter 43.701937 m, the row the table prints at 0.02 m.", "UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS, 3000.000000 m2, equivalent diameter 61.803872 m.", "UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS, 6000.000000 m2, equivalent diameter 87.403874 m, since half a centimetre is the default film.", "CONFINED, 400.000000 m2, since the thickness is read as a bund depth."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The thickness table prints 3000.000000 m2 and 61.803872 m at 0.01 m, from A = V / delta and D = sqrt(4 V / (pi delta)). 1500.000000 m2 and 43.701937 m are the 0.02 m row. 6000.000000 m2 and 87.403874 m are the 0.005 m row, and the engine has no default thickness at all. A stated thickness gives an unconfined pool.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 4, 'The stated thickness of the 30 m3 pool is halved from 0.01 m to 0.005 m. What happens to the equivalent diameter?', '["It doubles, from 61.803872 m, because the area doubles when the thickness is halved and the diameter follows the area.", "It halves, from 61.803872 to 27.639532 m, since a thinner pool is a smaller pool on the ground.", "It is unchanged at 61.803872 m, because the diameter depends on the volume alone and the thickness only sets the depth.", "It rises by 1.414214, from 61.803872 to 87.403874 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 8: the diameter goes as one over the square root of the thickness, so halving it raises the diameter by, derived, 1.414214, from 61.803872 to 87.403874 m. The AREA doubles, from 3000.000000 to 6000.000000 m2, but the diameter goes as the square root of the area. A thinner pool covers more ground, and 27.639532 m belongs to 0.05 m. The thickness enters D = sqrt(4 V / (pi delta)) directly.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 5, 'The engine has no spreading model. Where does the thickness of an unconfined pool come from?', '["The analyst states it, and the pool the engine returns is only as good as that stated thickness.", "The engine spreads the spill until it reaches a minimum film thickness it holds for each kind of ground.", "It is read from the Yellow Book table for the substance.", "The engine derives it from the spill volume, by solving the Yellow Book spreading equation for a pool that is fed until it stops."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 8 says no spreading model is implemented and the thickness is the analyst''s, with the model string "A = V / delta with the stated thickness; D = sqrt(4 V / (pi delta))". The engine holds no minimum film thickness and reads no thickness table. It does not solve a spreading equation: that is exactly the model it does not implement.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 6, 'The Yellow Book pool example (V 28.3 m3, delta 0.02 m) prints D = 42.445 m and A = 1415 m2. What does the engine give, and why the small difference?', '["43.701937 m and 1500.000000 m2, since the engine works from a 30 m3 spill at that thickness.", "42.445 m and 1415 m2 exactly, rounded to the book''s digits.", "42.445659 m and 1415.000000 m2; the book prints its values truncated.", "42.445659 m, from a different value of pi, with a warning."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 8: the engine gives a diameter of 42.445659 m and an area of 1415.000000 m2, and the book prints its values truncated. 43.701937 m and 1500.000000 m2 are the 30 m3 teaching spill at 0.02 m, a different volume. The engine returns its full value, and nothing about pi or a warning is involved.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 7, 'The engine returns `containment` on every pool. Which value does it carry for a pool of stated thickness?', '["CONFINED, with the stated thickness recorded in the basis block beside the bund area it replaces.", "UNCONFINED_SPREAD, since the pool spreads until it reaches the thickness the analyst typed for it.", "OVERTOPPED, since a pool with no bund is one that has spilled past every wall there is.", "UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 8 tables `containment` as UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS on every row of the thickness table and CONFINED for the bund. The label says the thickness was stated, because nothing spreads. An overtopping spill is refused, so there is no OVERTOPPED result, and CONFINED belongs to a bund floor.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 8, 'The hexane-like pool (10 m, wind at 10 m of 3 m/s, vapour pressure 16000 Pa, 0.08618 kg/mol, 293.15 K) evaporates by Mackay and Matsugu. What rate does the engine return?', '["0.010163820387 kg/s, the mass transfer coefficient.", "0.451595 kg/s, from an evaporation flux of 0.005749887804 kg/(m2 s) over 78.539816 m2.", "0.005749887804 kg/s, the evaporation flux alone.", "0.672653 kg/s, the rate the wind sweep prints at 5 m/s, since Mackay and Matsugu uses the wind at 2 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 9 prints the rate as 0.451595 kg/s, the evaporation flux 0.005749887804 kg/(m2 s) times the pool area 78.539816 m2. 0.010163820387 is the mass transfer coefficient in m/s. The model string ends "x A", so the area enters. 0.672653 kg/s is the 5 m/s row; the argument is the wind at 10 m, `windSpeed10mMS`.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 9, 'The wind at 10 m over the hexane-like pool is doubled from 1 to 2 m/s. Why does the rate go from 0.191688 to only 0.329153 kg/s?', '["The rate grows with the wind to the power 0.78, so doubling the wind less than doubles it.", "The rate is linear in the wind, and the pool cools as the wind rises, which pulls the vapour pressure down.", "The rate is capped by the vapour pressure.", "The rate grows with the square root of the wind, as the evaporation flux of a pool does in the Yellow Book spreading model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mass transfer coefficient is km = 0.004786 u10^0.78 (2r)^-0.11 Sc^-0.67, and section 9 says the rate grows with the wind to the power 0.78. The vapour pressure is a stated input the engine never adjusts, so there is no cooling and no cap. The power is 0.78, and the engine has no spreading model.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 10, 'The hexane-like pool grows from 10 m to 40 m across. What happens to the evaporation flux and to the rate?', '["Both stay proportional to the area, so the evaporation flux is unchanged at 0.005749887804 kg/(m2 s) and the rate rises.", "The evaporation flux rises to 0.006863506842 kg/(m2 s) as the pool widens, and the rate rises faster than the area.", "The evaporation flux falls to 0.004936654932 kg/(m2 s) and the rate falls to 0.021562 kg/s.", "The evaporation flux falls slowly, to 0.004936654932 kg/(m2 s), while the rate rises to 6.203584 kg/s with the area."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 9: the diameter enters to the power minus 0.11, so the evaporation flux FALLS slowly as the pool grows, to 0.004936654932 kg/(m2 s) at 40 m, while the rate still grows with the area, to 6.203584 kg/s. 0.006863506842 kg/(m2 s) is the 2 m pool, the largest evaporation flux in the table. 0.021562 kg/s is the rate of the 2 m pool.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 11, 'What Schmidt number does the evaporation call use when none is typed, and what constant leads the mass transfer coefficient?', '["0.67, the exponent in the correlation, and a constant of 0.78, the exponent on the wind.", "0.8, and a constant of 0.010163820387, the mass transfer coefficient of the hexane-like pool.", "0.8, the Yellow Book value for gases and vapours in general, and `MACKAY_MATSUGU_C` of 0.004786.", "1, and no constant."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 9: the Schmidt number defaults to 0.8, the Yellow Book''s value for gases and vapours in general, and `MACKAY_MATSUGU_C` is 0.004786. The 0.67 and 0.78 are exponents in km = 0.004786 u10^0.78 (2r)^-0.11 Sc^-0.67. 0.010163820387 m/s is a result of the correlation for one pool.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 12, 'An evaporation call is made with a wind at 10 m of zero. Why does the engine refuse it?', '["A still pool does not evaporate at all, so the engine refuses rather than return a rate of zero it would have to warn about.", "The correlation gives zero evaporation in calm air, which is its form and not physics, so the engine refuses on `windSpeed10mMS`.", "The wind enters as a divisor.", "The engine needs the stability class for calm air."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words are "windSpeed10mMS: must be above 0 m/s: the correlation gives zero evaporation in calm air, which is its form and not physics". A real pool does evaporate in still air, and the refusal says the zero is a property of the correlation. The wind enters km to the power 0.78, as a multiplier. No stability class is involved in the evaporation call.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 13, 'A pool is typed with a vapour pressure above ambient. What does the engine say?', '["It computes the evaporation rate with the vapour pressure capped at ambient, 101325 Pa, and warns that the pool may be close to its boiling point.", "It computes the evaporation rate as normal, since Mackay and Matsugu holds for any vapour pressure above zero and the pool itself is not modelled.", "\"windSpeed10mMS: must be above 0 m/s: the correlation gives zero evaporation in calm air, which is its form and not physics\"", "\"vapourPressurePa: is at or above ambient: the pool is boiling, and this non-boiling evaporation model does not apply\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 9: two refusals draw the model''s edge, and a vapour pressure at or above ambient is refused on `vapourPressurePa` in the quoted words, because Mackay and Matsugu is a model for a pool below its boiling point. A refusal carries no number, so nothing is capped or computed. The calm air message is the other refusal.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 14, 'Why is the Mackay and Matsugu evaporation rate taught in this course and never graded?', '["It is a single route quantity: no second derivation and no public worked number stands behind it, only the transcription from the Yellow Book.", "It is graded in the Professional tier, since the evaporation rate feeds the pool fire.", "Its output is a rate in kg/s, which the course grades only for a hole.", "The oracle disagrees with the engine by more than the tolerance, so it cannot be used for a graded answer anywhere in this course."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 9 says the correlation has no second derivation and no public worked number behind it in the engine''s validation record: the only check is the transcription from the Yellow Book. It is taught and never a graded answer, in any tier. No oracle disagreement is recorded, and a rate in kg/s is graded for the outflow through a hole.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pools-and-evaporation', 15, 'What does the engine''s model string say it does with a spill in a bund?', '["It spreads the spill across the bund floor until it reaches the stated thickness, and returns the wetted part of the floor.", "It fills the bund to the height of its wall and reports the volume held back, with the excess refused.", "\"pool covers the bund floor; D = sqrt(4 A / pi)\"", "It covers the bund floor, D = sqrt(4 V / (pi delta))."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is the model string for a bund, verbatim, from section 8: the pool covers the whole floor and its equivalent diameter comes from the floor area. A bund takes no thickness, since the floor sets the area. The depth is volume over area and never the wall height. sqrt(4 V / (pi delta)) is the stated thickness form.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 1, 'Which Pasquill-Gifford class is the most stable, which the most unstable, and which is neutral?', '["A is the most stable, because its sigmas are the largest and the plume holds together, and F the most unstable, with D neutral.", "F is the most stable (a clear night, light wind), A the most unstable (a sunny afternoon, light wind), and D is neutral.", "F is the most stable, A the most unstable, and C is neutral, since C sits in the middle of the six letters.", "D is the most stable, being neutral, and A and F are both unstable at the two ends of the table the engine exports."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 10: class A is the most unstable (a sunny afternoon, light wind) and class F the most stable (a clear night, light wind); D is neutral. A has the LARGEST sigmas because an unstable atmosphere spreads the plume fastest, so large sigmas mean strong mixing. Neutral is D, whatever its place in the alphabet.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 2, 'What sigma_z does the engine return for class D at 1000 m?', '["55.950288 m, the vertical spread the ALOHA note calls the misprint.", "76.277007 m, the crosswind spread in the same class at the same distance.", "23.076923 m, from the class E column at the same 1000 m.", "37.947332 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sigma_z table prints 37.947332 m for class D at 1000 m, with the engine''s sz2 of 0.0015. 55.950288 m is what the misprinted 0.00015 would give, derived for comparison and never returned. 76.277007 m is sigma_y, the crosswind spread, for class D at 1000 m. 23.076923 m is class E.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 3, 'ALOHA prints the class D sz2 as 0.0015 and notes that a value printed since Briggs 1973 is incorrect. Which does the engine carry?', '["0.00015, because Briggs 1973 is the original source of the rural curves and the engine follows the original over any later table.", "Both, and it reports the larger vertical spread as a warning on every class D result it returns to the analyst.", "0.0015, the ALOHA value; the 0.00015 printed since Briggs 1973 is the misprint.", "0.0003, the class E and F value."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 10: the engine carries 0.0015. ALOHA notes that 0.00015, printed since Briggs 1973, is incorrect; at 1000 m the two would give 37.947332 and 55.950288 m. The engine carries one coefficient and no warning compares them. 0.0003 is sz2 for classes E and F.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 4, 'The Briggs sigmas are asked for at 60 m, below the usual range. What does the engine do?', '["It returns them with a warning that the distance is outside 100 m to 10 km and the result should be treated as an extrapolation.", "It refuses, because the sigmas are undefined below 100 m.", "It returns the sigmas at 100 m instead, the nearest distance inside the range the curves are quoted for.", "It returns them with no comment."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 10: outside 100 m to 10 km the sigmas carry a warning and are still returned: "the downwind distance is outside 100 m to 10 km, the range these curves are usually quoted for; treat the result as an extrapolation". The table marks 60 m and 20000 m "yes". Nothing is refused or clamped to 100 m, and the warning is not silent.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 5, 'The UBIT release is 2 kg/s of carbon monoxide in a 3 m/s wind, class D. At ground level on the centreline from a ground level release, what is the concentration at 500 m?', '["73.313504 mg/m3, the reading the engine gives at 1000 m on the same centreline.", "1562.900663 mg/m3, the reading of the same release under class F.", "209.377772 mg/m3, since the engine prints the same figure whether the unit is ppm or mg/m3.", "239.712839 mg/m3, or 209.377772 ppm."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The UBIT table prints 239.712839 mg/m3 and 209.377772 ppm at 500 m in class D. 73.313504 mg/m3 is the 1000 m row. 1562.900663 mg/m3 is class F at 500 m. 209.377772 is the same concentration in ppm, and ppm and mg/m3 differ by the molar mass over the molar volume.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 6, 'Why does the plume''s ground level centreline concentration from a ground level release come out as Q / (pi sy sz u)?', '["Because the engine drops the second exponential at the ground, so the expression loses its factor of two.", "Both exponentials in the reflected expression equal one there, so the image source at minus h doubles what an unbounded plume would give.", "Because the sigmas are set to one at ground level.", "Because the ground absorbs the plume, which halves the unbounded result, and the two in the denominator cancels against it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 11: at ground level on the centreline from a ground level release the two exponentials are both one, and the reflection DOUBLES what an unbounded plume would give, turning Q / (2 pi sy sz u) into Q / (pi sy sz u). The engine keeps the image source, so nothing is dropped. The sigmas depend on the class and distance. Total ground reflection is the opposite of absorption.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 7, 'At 500 m the UBIT release gives 1562.900663 mg/m3 in class F. How does that compare with class D, and why?', '["6.519887 times class D: the stable night-time plume stays thin and near the ground.", "19.767914 mg/m3 is higher still, because the unstable class A plume mixes the gas down to the ground fastest.", "About the same as class D, since the release rate and the wind are unchanged and the class only moves the plume sideways.", "6.519887 times class D, because class F has the fastest wind."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 11 says class F gives the highest ground level concentration at 500 m, derived 6.519887 times class D (239.712839 mg/m3), because the plume stays thin and near the ground. 19.767914 mg/m3 is class A and is the LOWEST in the table. The class sets both sigmas, so it changes the concentration a great deal. The wind is 3 m/s in every row.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 8, 'The UBIT wind is doubled with the class and distance held. What does the plume do to the concentration, and what happens as the wind falls to zero?', '["It doubles, since a stronger wind carries more of the gas from the release to the receptor in the same time.", "It is unchanged, because the Briggs sigmas already carry the wind, so at zero the engine returns the class F value.", "It halves, since the concentration is inversely proportional to the wind at fixed sigmas; at zero the engine refuses, having no calm air form.", "It halves, and at zero it returns the largest concentration the table carries, from class F."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 11: the concentration is inversely proportional to the wind speed at fixed sigmas, and the plume has no calm air form; the engine''s words are "windSpeedMS: must be above 0 m/s: the Gaussian plume divides by the wind speed and has no calm-air form". A stronger wind dilutes the gas into more air. The sigmas depend on class and distance only. A refusal carries no number, so nothing comes back from class F.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 9, 'The Purple Book worked case (Q 100 kg/s, u 5 m/s, h = z = 1 m, sigma_y 28.8 m, sigma_z 10.3 m) prints C = 21.3 g/m3. What does the engine give, and how does it take the sigmas?', '["21.3 g/m3 exactly, with the sigmas looked up from class D at the printed distance of 1000 m.", "21.260627 mg/m3, since the engine reports every plume concentration in mg/m3 and the book in g/m3.", "21.260627 g/m3, with the sigmas given explicitly in place of a class.", "21.260627 g/m3, with the sigmas computed from class F by Briggs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 11: the engine gives 21.260627 g/m3 against the printed 21.3 g/m3, and the worked case is at z = h = 1 m with the sigmas given explicitly, which the engine accepts in place of a class. No class lookup is made, so there is no class D or class F in it. The figure is 21.260627 g/m3: the book case is in g/m3, and reading it as mg/m3 is a thousandfold error.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 10, 'For eight plume cases the oracle integrates u times the concentration over every crosswind distance and every height above the ground. What does that second route show?', '["It recovers the release rate, a ratio of 1 to within one part in a billion; a plume that dropped the reflection would return a half.", "It recovers twice the release rate, since the image source adds a second plume of its own below the ground.", "It recovers the concentration at 500 m in class D, 239.712839 mg/m3, independently of the engine''s own code.", "It recovers the release rate only in class D."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 11 describes the mass flux integral: the golden records the recovered release rate over the true one, which is 1 to within one part in a billion in every case. The reflection keeps all the gas above the ground, which is why it recovers exactly Q and why dropping it would return a half. The integral recovers a rate and says nothing about one concentration, and it holds for all eight cases.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 11, '400 ppm of carbon monoxide (28.01 g/mol) is converted to mg/m3 at 101325 Pa. What does the engine return at 298.15 K, and at 273.15 K?', '["499.866834 at 298.15 K and 457.952795 mg/m3 at 273.15 K, since a warmer gas carries more mass in each cubic metre.", "465.763690 mg/m3 at both, because the engine converts at 293.15 K whatever temperature the call states.", "608.192135 at 273.15 K, since the conversion uses the molar mass of hydrogen sulphide.", "457.952795 at 298.15 K and 499.866834 mg/m3 at 273.15 K."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The section 12 table prints 457.952795 mg/m3 at 298.15 K and 499.866834 mg/m3 at 273.15 K for 400 ppm of carbon monoxide. A colder gas is denser, so the same ppm is MORE mg/m3 when cold. The engine converts at the STATED temperature, and 465.763690 is the 293.15 K column. 608.192135 is hydrogen sulphide at 273.15 K.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 12, 'What molar volume does the engine use at 298.15 K and 101325 Pa, and how does it relate to the CCOHS 24.45 L/mol?', '["24.45 L/mol exactly, since the engine takes the CCOHS figure as a constant for every conversion it makes.", "24.465404 L/mol from R T / P; the printed 24.45 lies within one part in a thousand of it.", "22.413970 L/mol, the molar volume at 273.15 K, which the engine uses for every temperature.", "24.055117 L/mol, at 293.15 K."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 12: the molar volume is the ideal gas R T / P at the stated temperature and pressure, 24.465404 L/mol at 298.15 K, and the CCOHS and NIOSH figure of 24.45 approximates it, gated at one part in a thousand. The engine computes it and holds no constant 24.45. 22.413970 and 24.055117 L/mol are the 273.15 K and 293.15 K rows.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 13, 'Hydrogen sulphide (34.08 g/mol) at 50 ppm reads 76.024017 mg/m3 at 273.15 K and 69.649372 mg/m3 at 298.15 K. What does that difference teach?', '["A ppm to mg/m3 conversion is only correct at the temperature it was made at, because a colder gas is denser.", "The engine''s two conversion functions disagree, since mgM3ToPpm uses a different molar volume from ppmToMgM3.", "Hydrogen sulphide changes molar mass with temperature.", "The warmer figure is the true one, and 273.15 K is used only for occupational limits in the CCOHS source."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 12: a colder gas is denser, so the same ppm is more mg/m3, and the molar volume is taken at the STATED temperature. The two functions are exact inverses at the same temperature and pressure, so they never disagree. The molar mass is a stated constant of the substance. Neither temperature is the true one; each is right at its own temperature.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 14, 'The plume returns mg/m3. When does it also return ppm, and what does `ppmToMgM3` do with no molar mass?', '["Always, since the plume carries the molar mass of carbon monoxide as its default and converts at 298.15 K.", "Only in class D, because the conversion assumes air of 28.01 g/mol whenever no molar mass at all is typed into the call by the analyst.", "Never: ppm comes only from `mgM3ToPpm` called by hand, and the missing molar mass is taken as 1 g/mol.", "Only when a molar mass is given; with none the conversion is refused: \"molarMassGMol: must be a molar mass above 0 g/mol\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 12: the plume returns mg/m3, and ppm only when a molar mass is given, and section 3 tables the refusal on `molarMassGMol` in the quoted words. The engine carries no default molar mass. 28.01 g/mol is carbon monoxide, a stated input of the UBIT release, and class plays no part in the unit.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-gaussian-plume', 15, 'Which spread does sigma_y describe, and which sigma_z, and what unit do both carry?', '["sigma_y is the vertical spread and sigma_z the crosswind spread, both in m.", "sigma_y is the downwind spread and sigma_z the vertical spread, both dimensionless fractions of the distance.", "sigma_y is the crosswind spread and sigma_z the vertical spread, both in m.", "Both are crosswind spreads in m, one for the near field and one for the far."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 10 says it plainly: sigma_y is the crosswind spread and sigma_z the vertical spread, both in m. Reversing them puts y and z on the wrong axes of the plume expression, where y is the crosswind distance and z the receptor height. Neither is a downwind spread, and neither is a fraction.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 1, 'The UBIT release, ground level source and receptor, class D. At what downwind distance does the centreline fall to 100 mg/m3?', '["830.322126 m, a single distance with the near distance null.", "2564.337939 m, the class F distance.", "331.629944 m, the 500 mg/m3 distance.", "656.636313 m, the stack''s far root."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The section 13 table prints REACHED at 830.322126 m for class D and 100 mg/m3, with a near distance of null. 2564.337939 m is class F at the same target. 331.629944 m is the class D distance to 500 mg/m3. 656.636313 m is the far root from the 25 m stack, a different release height.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 2, 'From a ground level release to a ground level receptor, why does the engine return only a far distance and a null near distance?', '["The engine searches outward only, and it reports the first crossing it meets as the far root.", "The near root lies inside 100 m, where the Briggs sigmas carry a warning, so the engine suppresses it and returns null in its place for safety.", "From the ground the concentration falls monotonically with distance, so a target is crossed once.", "The near root is always at the source, which the plume cannot evaluate, so it is set to null."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 13: from the ground the concentration falls monotonically, so there is one distance. The null is not a suppressed value: there is no second crossing to report. The engine finds roots by bisection and does not hide a root because of the sigma warning, and the source itself is not a root of anything.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 3, 'Class F against class D for the UBIT release to 20 mg/m3 from the ground: what are the two distances?', '["2265.987260 m in class F against 9384.252298 m in class D, since the stable class disperses the gas faster and so reaches the target closer in.", "2564.337939 m in class F against 830.322126 m in class D, the distances the table gives for the same pair of classes.", "9384.252298 m in both, since the target sets the distance and the class only sets how quickly the plume gets there.", "9384.252298 m in class F against 2265.987260 m in class D."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The table prints 9384.252298 m for class F and 2265.987260 m for class D at 20 mg/m3. The stable class F keeps the plume thin and near the ground, so it reaches FARTHER; the reversed pair has the classes backwards. 2564.337939 and 830.322126 m are the two classes at 100 mg/m3. The class changes the sigmas and so the distance.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 4, 'From a 25 m stack, receptor at ground level, class D, the target is 100 mg/m3. What does the engine return, and why two distances?', '["REACHED at 830.322126 m only, the same as the ground level release, since the stack changes nothing at the ground.", "REACHED at 221.924194 m and 656.636313 m: the concentration rises from nothing, peaks and falls, so a target below the peak is met twice.", "NOT_REACHED, since the peak at the ground from a stack is 153.887548 mg/m3.", "REACHED at 150.672013 m and 2151.227430 m, the two roots of a stack plume."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stack table prints REACHED with a near distance of 221.924194 m and a far distance of 656.636313 m for 100 mg/m3: from a stack the ground concentration rises, peaks at 153.887548 mg/m3 and falls. 830.322126 m is the ground level release. The peak lies ABOVE 100 mg/m3, so the target is reached. 150.672013 and 2151.227430 m are the roots for 20 mg/m3.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 5, 'The same 25 m stack is given a target of 500 mg/m3. What does the engine report?', '["REACHED at 331.629944 m, the class D distance to 500 mg/m3 that the ground level release gives on the same centreline.", "BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE, since the engine searched as far as it could and never found the target.", "A refusal, since a target above the peak is an invalid input to `plumeDistanceToConcentration` and must be lowered.", "NOT_REACHED: the peak is 153.887548 mg/m3 at 347.557177 m, below the target, and both distances are null."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 13: NOT_REACHED means the peak is below the target. The stack''s peak is 153.887548 mg/m3 at 347.557177 m and both distances are null. 331.629944 m belongs to the ground level release. BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE is the other state, for a plume still ABOVE the target where the search stops. A target above the peak is a valid question with an answer, so it is not refused.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 6, 'What does BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE mean, and what does the engine do for class F to 20 mg/m3 with the search capped at 2000 m?', '["The plume is still above the target at the largest distance searched; the engine says so and returns a far distance of null instead of the cap.", "The peak is below the target, so no distance meets it; the far distance is set to the 2000 m cap as a bound.", "The distance lies past 10 km, outside the Briggs range, so the engine refuses the call.", "The engine returns 2000 m as the far distance, flagged."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 13: BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE means still above the target at the largest distance searched, and for class F to 20 mg/m3 with a 2000 m cap it returns a far distance of null. The engine says so instead of returning the cap, so 2000 m is never reported as an answer. A peak below the target is NOT_REACHED. The uncapped class F distance, 9384.252298 m, is inside 10 km, and nothing is refused.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 7, 'The engine names three states for a distance search. Which one says the peak concentration never reaches the target?', '["BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE, because the target lies beyond where the plume can carry the gas at that concentration.", "REACHED with both the near distance and the far distance null, since no crossing of the target was found.", "NOT_REACHED", "SUBSONIC, the state for a plume whose peak never reaches the target."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 13 names the three: REACHED, NOT_REACHED (the peak is below the target) and BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE (still above the target at the largest distance searched). BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE is the opposite case, a plume that is too strong where the search stops. REACHED always carries a distance. SUBSONIC is a gas outflow regime from section 6.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 8, 'The distance search returns 830.322126 m for class D and 100 mg/m3. How can the result be checked with the plume itself?', '["Run the plume at 830.322126 m and expect 239.712839 mg/m3, the class D reading at 500 m.", "Run the plume at 830.322126 m: it gives 100.000000 mg/m3, the target, which is the round trip.", "Halve the distance and expect twice the target.", "Compare it with the Purple Book worked case, which prints the distance to 100 mg/m3 for this release in class D at a wind of 3 m/s."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 13: the round trip is that the plume at the far distance returns the target, and at 830.322126 m it gives 100.000000 mg/m3. 239.712839 mg/m3 is the 500 m reading, a different distance. The concentration is not inversely proportional to the distance, since the sigmas grow with distance at their own rates. The Purple Book case is a single concentration at stated sigmas and prints no distance.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 9, 'A receptor sits 50 m to the side of the centreline, 500 m downwind of the UBIT release in class D. What does it see?', '["105.544135 mg/m3, 0.440294 of the centreline.", "210.227800 mg/m3, 0.876998 of the centreline, the reading the crosswind table gives for a receptor at 20 m.", "239.712839 mg/m3, the centreline itself.", "9.008708 mg/m3, 0.037581 of the centreline."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The crosswind table prints 105.544135 mg/m3 at 50 m, 0.440294 of the centreline''s 239.712839 mg/m3. 210.227800 mg/m3 is the 20 m row and 9.008708 mg/m3 the 100 m row. 239.712839 mg/m3 is the centreline itself, and the plume falls away from it as exp(-y^2 / 2 sigma_y^2) at every crosswind distance.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 10, 'The distance search reports 830.322126 m for 100 mg/m3 in class D. A receptor stands 100 m to the side at that downwind distance. What does the analyst need to know?', '["The distance already allows for the crosswind position, since the engine searches along the edge of the plume at one sigma_y.", "The receptor sees exactly the target, because the plume is uniform across its width at any given downwind distance.", "The receptor sees more, since the crosswind term adds a second exponential to the plume that is always greater than one.", "The distance to a concentration is always a centreline distance, and a receptor to the side sees less."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 13: the ratio to the centreline is exp(-y^2 / 2 sigma_y^2), and the distance to a concentration is ALWAYS a centreline distance; a receptor to the side sees less. At 500 m in class D the ratio 100 m to the side is 0.037581. The search runs along the centreline, the plume is not uniform across its width, and the crosswind factor never exceeds one.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 11, 'Which method string does `plumeDistanceToConcentration` report?', '["\"inverse of the Gaussian plume in closed form, Briggs rural sigmas; exact\"", "\"root of the ground-reflected Gaussian plume centreline concentration, Briggs rural sigmas; bisection\"", "\"interpolation in a table of the ground-reflected Gaussian plume centreline concentration at fixed distances\"", "\"pool covers the bund floor; D = sqrt(4 A / pi)\""]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 13 quotes the method string verbatim: the engine finds the root of the ground-reflected centreline concentration by bisection, using the Briggs rural sigmas. There is no closed form inverse and no interpolation in a table. The bund string belongs to `poolFromSpill`.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 12, 'Someone asks the distance search for a target of zero. Which message answers?', '["BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE, since the plume is still above zero at the largest distance searched.", "NOT_REACHED with both distances null, because a concentration of zero is never reached at any finite distance.", "\"targetConcentrationMgM3: must be a concentration above 0 mg/m3\"", "REACHED at the cap."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 3 tables this refusal on `targetConcentrationMgM3` in those words. A refusal carries no number and no state, so none of the three search states comes back. The argument name also says the target is in mg/m3.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 13, 'The receptor is placed right at the release point, 0 m downwind. Which refusal comes back?', '["It returns the release rate divided by the wind speed, the concentration at the point of release.", "It returns the 100 m result with a warning that the distance is outside 100 m to 10 km, the range the curves are quoted for.", "\"sigmaYM: a stability class or a sigma_y above 0 m is required\"", "\"downwindDistanceM: must be a downwind distance above 0 m: the plume is undefined at the source\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 3 gives this refusal on `downwindDistanceM` in those words: the plume is undefined at the source. A refusal carries no number, so nothing is computed at 0 m and nothing is moved to 100 m. The `sigmaYM` message is the refusal for a call with no class and no sigmas.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 14, 'A plume call gives neither a stability class nor a sigma_y. Which field does the engine name?', '["`sigmaYM`: \"sigmaYM: a stability class or a sigma_y above 0 m is required\"", "`stabilityClass`, with the message listing A, B, C, D, E, F as the classes the table carries.", "`windSpeedMS`, since without a class the engine cannot tell the wind.", "`downwindDistanceM`, since the sigmas depend on the distance and cannot be formed without it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 3 tables this call, no class and no sigmas, on `sigmaYM` in the quoted words: the plume accepts sigmas in place of a class, as the Purple Book case shows, so it asks for one or the other. The `stabilityClass` message is for a class the table does not carry. The wind and the distance are separate inputs with their own refusals.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-how-far-the-plume-reaches', 15, 'From the 25 m stack in class D, where on the ground is the concentration highest, and how high is it?', '["At the foot of the stack.", "At 347.557177 m, where it peaks at 153.887548 mg/m3.", "At 221.924194 m, reaching 100 mg/m3.", "At 656.636313 m, the far root."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the stack the ground reading climbs from nothing, tops out at 153.887548 mg/m3 when 347.557177 m downwind, then declines, so the base of the stack sees the least of it. 221.924194 and 656.636313 m are where the rising and falling limbs cross 100 mg/m3.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A reviewer asks the engine to report how often the AMENAM line will leak. What should the analyst tell them?', '["The engine reports a leak frequency in the basis block of every outflow result, beside the model and the source.", "The engine computes effects only: it says what happens if the release occurs, and how often it occurs belongs elsewhere.", "The engine derives the frequency from the hole diameter, since a larger hole is a rarer event on any line.", "The frequency is the mass rate divided by the inventory."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 1: the engine computes effects and never frequencies, and a consequence model says what happens if the release occurs; how often it occurs belongs elsewhere. The basis block carries the model, its source and its units, and no frequency. Nothing in the engine turns a hole size or a mass rate into a frequency.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'A vessel pressure is known in bar gauge. The engine never converts a unit it was not asked to. What must the analyst do before typing it?', '["Convert it to pascals and add the atmosphere, because a name ending in Pa means pascals and an absolute pressure.", "Type the bar figure as it stands, since the engine reads the unit from the value and converts it on the way in.", "Convert it to pascals only, since the engine adds `ATM_PA` itself to any upstream pressure.", "Type it into a field whose name ends in Bar."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The unit lives in the argument name, and Pa there means pascals measured from vacuum. So the bar reading has to become pascals, and the atmosphere the gauge leaves out has to go back in; skip that and the call runs one atmosphere short. The value itself carries no unit the engine could read, `ATM_PA` is ambient and never an addition, and no name uses Bar.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, '`ATM_PA` is one of the exported constants. What is it, and when does the engine use it?', '["9.80665, the gravitational acceleration.", "100000 Pa, one bar, which the engine takes as ambient whenever no pressure is typed for it.", "101325 Pa, the ambient pressure unless a call states otherwise.", "101325 Pa, added to every upstream pressure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 1 prints `ATM_PA` as 101325, and section 2 says ambient is `ATM_PA`, 101325 Pa, unless a call states otherwise. 9.80665 is `G_M_S2`. 100000 is the upper end of `BAGSTER_RANGE_PA_M`, which has nothing to do with ambient. Every pressure is typed absolute, so nothing is added to it.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Which statement about a refusal from this engine is true?', '["It carries the result at the nearest valid input.", "It carries a number flagged as unreliable.", "It carries a partial result.", "It carries no number: it holds the engine''s message and the field it names."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 3 says each refusal is the engine''s `error` string with the field it names, and that a refusal carries no number. Nothing is computed at a nearby input, nothing is flagged and returned, and no partial result is kept.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Which of the engine''s named sources fixes the pool diameter and the Mackay and Matsugu evaporation?', '["PB, the TNO Purple Book.", "ALOHA, the NOAA documentation.", "CCOHS, the conversion source.", "YB, the TNO Yellow Book CPR 14E (2005)."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The YB row of the sources table covers outflow through a hole, the pool diameter and Mackay and Matsugu, so the pool and its evaporation are Yellow Book work. PB is where the worked carbon monoxide plume comes from. ALOHA is the dispersion source. CCOHS is only the unit conversion, with nothing to say about a pool.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'The engine names the TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999). What does it use that source for among the Associate material?', '["The Briggs rural sigma coefficients for the six Pasquill-Gifford classes.", "A worked carbon monoxide plume, which the golden reproduces as a published check.", "The molar volume of 24.45 L/mol used to convert between the two concentration units.", "The subsonic outflow coefficient psi used in the gas outflow model once the flow stops being choked."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 4 lists a worked carbon monoxide plume among what the Purple Book fixes, the case section 11 reproduces at 21.260627 g/m3. The Briggs coefficients come from ALOHA, the molar volume from CCOHS, and psi from the Yellow Book.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'The exported constant `BRIGGS_ADVISORY_RANGE_M` holds 100 to 10000. What happens to a plume asked for outside it?', '["The sigmas are returned with a warning to treat the result as an extrapolation.", "The call is refused on `downwindDistanceM`, naming the range the Briggs curves are quoted for.", "The distance is clamped to the nearest end of the range and the result is computed there.", "Nothing: the constant is documentation only."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The name says advisory, and that is how it acts: at 60 m or 20000 m the sigma table marks a warning and still prints the spreads. The only distance refusal belongs to 0 m, the distance typed is the distance used, and the warning text is the constant at work.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Only 1 m of crude now stands over the hole and the blanket is unchanged. Which driving pressure and rate does the sweep print?', '["A driving pressure of 98675.000000 Pa and 15.767020 kg/s, the figures for a head of 0 m.", "A driving pressure of 107010.652500 Pa and 16.419486 kg/s.", "123681.957500 Pa and 17.652215 kg/s.", "11.225132 kg/s."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The head sweep prints 107010.652500 Pa and 16.419486 kg/s at 1 m. 98675.000000 Pa and 15.767020 kg/s are the 0 m row, and 123681.957500 Pa and 17.652215 kg/s the 3 m row. 11.225132 kg/s is the ambient ullage row of the other sweep, with the head at 6 m.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The AMENAM ullage at 150000 Pa gives 15.768131 kg/s; the head at 0 m with the 200000 Pa ullage gives 15.767020 kg/s. Why are the two so close?', '["Both are choked, so both pass the same rate.", "Their driving pressures are almost equal, 98688.915000 against 98675.000000 Pa, and the rate follows the driving pressure alone.", "They are the same case printed twice, with a rounding difference in the sixth decimal between the two sweeps.", "The discharge coefficient changes between the two sweeps and happens to compensate for the change in pressure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mass rate depends on the driving pressure P minus ambient. The ullage sweep prints 98688.915000 Pa at 150000 Pa, and the head sweep prints 98675.000000 Pa at 0 m, so the rates nearly agree. A liquid through a hole is never choked in this model, the two cases differ in both head and ullage, and the discharge coefficient is held at 0.62 throughout.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'The AMENAM hole is shrunk to 0.01 m with everything else held. What area and mass rate does the engine print?', '["0.000490873852 m2 and 4.838663 kg/s, which is the row the hole sweep prints for 0.025 m.", "0.001963495408 m2 and 19.354651 kg/s, since the hole enters only through the discharge coefficient.", "0.007853981634 m2 and 77.418603 kg/s, the row for a hole of 0.1 m, ten times the size.", "0.000078539816 m2 and 0.774186 kg/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The hole sweep prints 0.000078539816 m2 and 0.774186 kg/s at 0.01 m. 0.000490873852 m2 and 4.838663 kg/s are the 0.025 m row. 0.001963495408 m2 and 19.354651 kg/s are the line''s own 0.05 m hole, and the rate is linear in the area. 0.007853981634 m2 and 77.418603 kg/s belong to 0.1 m.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Suppose the analyst types 0.6 for Cd on the crude line. Which rate comes back, and in what way does Cd enter it?', '["19.354651 kg/s, replacing any coefficient below 0.62.", "24.973743 kg/s, since a smaller coefficient passes more.", "18.730307 kg/s, since the rate goes as the square root of the coefficient, the same as it does for the driving pressure.", "18.730307 kg/s; the rate is linear in the discharge coefficient."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The coefficient sweep prints 18.730307 kg/s at 0.6, and section 5 says the rate is linear in the discharge coefficient. The engine uses the typed coefficient and replaces nothing. 24.973743 kg/s is the 0.8 row, larger because a larger coefficient passes more. The square root holds the driving pressure and the density.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'At what ullage pressure does the AMENAM line reach 48.888640 kg/s with its 6 m head?', '["500000 Pa, a driving pressure of 448688.915000 Pa, since the rate is linear in the ullage above ambient.", "200000 Pa, the stated blanket.", "1000000 Pa, a driving pressure of 948688.915000 Pa.", "10000000 Pa, where the gas line reaches its highest choked rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ullage sweep prints 48.888640 kg/s at 1000000 Pa, with a driving pressure of 948688.915000 Pa. 500000 Pa gives 33.621626 kg/s, and the rate follows the square root of the driving pressure. 200000 Pa gives 19.354651 kg/s. 10000000 Pa is a row of the methane line in section 6, a different model.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'An analyst has a hole measured as an area and no diameter. What does the liquid outflow function accept?', '["The area, as `holeAreaM2`; its refusal for a zero diameter names the area as the alternative.", "Only a diameter, so the analyst must back-calculate one first.", "The area, but only in cm2, as `holeAreaCm2`.", "Neither without a discharge coefficient of one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bracket in the zero diameter message, "(or holeAreaM2)", is the engine telling the analyst that an area in square metres will do. Nothing needs back-calculating, no name takes square centimetres, and Cd is its own input whatever its value.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'The AMENAM line carries 850 kg/m3 crude, 6 m of head and a 200000 Pa ullage. Where does the pressure at the hole of 250013.915000 Pa come from?', '["The static head rhoL g hL plus the 200000 Pa above the liquid, all absolute.", "The 200000 Pa ullage plus one atmosphere, 101325 Pa, since the ullage is typed as gauge.", "The driving pressure 148688.915000 Pa plus the static head.", "The ullage times the density over the head."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 5: P = rhoL g hL + PaL is the absolute pressure at the hole, the static head plus the pressure above the liquid. The ullage is stated absolute, so no atmosphere is added. The driving pressure is P minus ambient, 148688.915000 Pa, and it already contains the head. The last formula is not the model.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'With the methane line held at 150000 Pa the pressure ratio reads 0.675500. How does the engine classify the flow, and at what rate?', '["CHOKED, psi 1.000000.", "SUBSONIC, psi 0.958958, 0.074275 kg/s.", "SUBSONIC, psi 0.756455.", "SUBSONIC, psi 0.999177."]'::jsonb, 1, '0.675500 sits well above 0.543927, so this row is subsonic, carrying psi 0.958958 and 0.074275 kg/s. Psi of 1.000000 only appears from 250000 Pa upward. 0.756455 is the row below it and 0.999177 the row above.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Push the methane teaching line to 2000000 Pa. Which rate and density appear in its row?', '["5.163609 kg/s at 64.305619 kg/m3, the rate and density printed for 10000000 Pa.", "0.258180 kg/s at 3.215281 kg/m3, which are the figures for 500000 Pa.", "0.129090 kg/s, the choked rate, which is the same at every upstream pressure once the hole chokes.", "1.032722 kg/s at 12.861124 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The table prints 1.032722 kg/s and an upstream density of 12.861124 kg/m3 at 2000000 Pa. 5.163609 kg/s belongs to 10000000 Pa and 0.258180 kg/s to 500000 Pa. A choked rate is linear in the upstream pressure, so it does not stay at the 250000 Pa figure.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'A gas with gamma of 1.2 leaks from the same hole into open air. Where does the engine put its choking threshold?', '["0.584679, the 1.1 row.", "0.564474, choking at 179503.418325 Pa.", "0.528282, the 1.4 row.", "0.543927, choking at 186284.176006 Pa, the figure for every gas."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reading across the gamma row for 1.2 gives 0.564474, and dividing ambient by it gives 179503.418325 Pa. The 1.1 and 1.4 rows hold the other two ratios offered. 0.543927 is methane''s own, at 1.31, and each gamma carries its own threshold.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'The AMENAM GAS line gives 0.129090 kg/s at 250000 Pa and 0.258180 kg/s at 500000 Pa. Why exactly twice?', '["The outflow coefficient psi doubles between the two rows, from 0.5 at 250000 Pa to one at 500000 Pa, and the rate follows it.", "The ratio has quadrupled, and the rate follows its square root.", "Both rows are choked, and a choked rate is linear in the upstream pressure.", "The discharge coefficient is doubled."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 6: both rows are CHOKED, and once choked the mass rate is LINEAR in the upstream pressure; doubling it doubles the rate, as the upstream density doubles from 1.607640 to 3.215281 kg/m3. Psi is 1.000000 on both rows. The pressure has doubled and the rate with it. The discharge coefficient is held at 0.62.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Of the two air cases straddling the boundary, the one with the smaller ratio, 0.52773437499999998, is air-barely-choked. How does the engine label it?', '["SUBSONIC, psi 0.999944960481, 0.112711 kg/s, since the ratio is within one part in a thousand of the critical value.", "CHOKED, 0.112711 kg/s.", "A refusal, too close to call.", "CHOKED, psi 1.000000000000, 0.113903 kg/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Air''s threshold is 0.52828178771717416, and this ratio falls just under it, so the label is choked, psi sits at exactly one and the rate is 0.113903 kg/s. 0.112711 kg/s is its subsonic neighbour. However near the boundary a case lands, the rule decides it and nothing is refused.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Set the yb-hydrogen-t0 case against the oracle''s nozzle maximisation route. What does the golden show?', '["15.31 kg/s from both, since the golden rounds each route to the printed digits of the Yellow Book before comparing them.", "15.292930 kg/s from the engine against 15.311760 kg/s from route B, the gap the inferred heat capacity ratio closes.", "SUBSONIC in the engine and CHOKED by route B.", "15.311760 kg/s from both, CHOKED, a relative difference of 5.80e-16."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 6 prints the yb-hydrogen-t0 row: CHOKED, the engine 15.311760 kg/s and route B 15.311760 kg/s, relative difference 5.80e-16. Nothing is rounded to the book''s digits. 15.292930 kg/s is what the engine gives at a heat capacity ratio of 1.4 and is no route B figure. The two routes agree on the regime.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Which upstream pressure puts the AMENAM GAS line exactly at its critical pressure ratio of 0.543927?', '["250000 Pa, the first choked row of the table.", "186284.176006 Pa, derived; the engine reports CHOKED there with psi 1.000000000000.", "180000 Pa, the last subsonic row, since the boundary lies at the last subsonic entry.", "208201.258009 Pa, where a gas with a heat capacity ratio of 1.67 chokes into the atmosphere."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 7 derives 186284.176006 Pa for the AMENAM line and reports CHOKED there with psi 1.000000000000. 250000 Pa and 180000 Pa are table rows on either side of the boundary. 208201.258009 Pa belongs to a heat capacity ratio of 1.67; the methane line''s is 1.31.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'The 30 m3 spill is given a stated thickness of 0.02 m. What area and equivalent diameter come back?', '["1415.000000 m2 and 42.445659 m, since the Yellow Book example uses the same thickness.", "600.000000 m2 and 27.639532 m, which are the figures printed for a stated thickness of 0.05 m.", "1500.000000 m2 and 43.701937 m.", "400.000000 m2 and 22.567583 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The thickness table prints 1500.000000 m2 and 43.701937 m at 0.02 m. The Yellow Book example shares the thickness but spills 28.3 m3, giving 1415.000000 m2 and 42.445659 m. 600.000000 m2 and 27.639532 m are the 0.05 m row. 400.000000 m2 and 22.567583 m are the bund.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'The confined pool covers a 400 m2 bund floor. What does its equivalent diameter of 22.567583 m describe?', '["The longest side of the bund, measured along its wall at the height the spill reaches.", "The distance the spill runs from the hole to the far wall of the bund before it comes to rest.", "The diameter of a circle with the same area as the bund floor, D = sqrt(4 A / pi).", "The depth of the pool times the area, 0.075000 m times 400 m2."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 8 gives the model string "pool covers the bund floor; D = sqrt(4 A / pi)": the equivalent diameter is that of a circle of the bund''s area. The bund''s shape is not an input, and the engine models no run of liquid across the floor: the pool simply covers it. Depth times area is the volume, 30 m3.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'What comparison decides whether `poolFromSpill` refuses a spill as overtopping its bund?', '["The spill volume against the bund''s volume, the floor area times a standard wall height of 1 m that the engine assumes.", "The depth the spill would stand at on the bund floor, volume over area, against the wall height.", "The equivalent diameter against the longest side of the bund, measured along the wall.", "The stated thickness against the wall height."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The bracket in the overtopping message names the test: depth above the wall height. The depth is volume over floor area, so the whole comparison is two heights. No wall height is assumed, a diameter is never compared with a side, and thickness belongs to the unbunded case.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'The 30 m3 spill is spread at a stated 0.05 m. What equivalent diameter does the engine return?', '["43.701937 m, the 0.02 m row.", "87.403874 m, the 0.005 m row.", "27.639532 m, over 600.000000 m2.", "22.567583 m, the bund''s equivalent diameter, because every pool of 30 m3 is the same size."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The thickness table prints 600.000000 m2 and 27.639532 m at 0.05 m. 43.701937 m is the 0.02 m row. 87.403874 m is the 0.005 m row: a THINNER pool is the wider one. 22.567583 m belongs to the 400 m2 bund; the thickness sets the area, so the same volume gives different sizes.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'The wind at 10 m over the hexane-like pool rises to 8 m/s. What evaporation rate does the engine print?', '["0.970521 kg/s of vapour.", "0.672653 kg/s, the rate the wind sweep prints at 5 m/s.", "0.451595 kg/s, since the wind enters only the mass transfer coefficient and leaves the rate unchanged.", "1.673771 kg/s, the rate the pool diameter sweep prints for a 20 m pool."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The wind sweep prints 0.970521 kg/s at 8 m/s. 0.672653 kg/s is the 5 m/s row. The mass transfer coefficient multiplies straight into the rate, so the wind raises it: 0.451595 kg/s is the 3 m/s case. 1.673771 kg/s is the 20 m pool in the diameter sweep.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'A 5 m hexane-like pool evaporates under the same conditions. What evaporation flux and rate does the engine print?', '["0.005749887804 kg/(m2 s), the 10 m pool.", "0.021562 kg/s, the 2 m pool.", "0.006205441275 kg/(m2 s) and 0.121844 kg/s.", "0.005749887804 kg/(m2 s) and 0.121844 kg/s, since the evaporation flux is the same for every pool size."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The diameter sweep prints 0.006205441275 kg/(m2 s) and 0.121844 kg/s at 5 m. 0.005749887804 and 0.451595 are the 10 m pool, and 0.006863506842 and 0.021562 the 2 m pool. The evaporation flux falls slowly as the pool grows, since the diameter enters to the power minus 0.11, so it is not the same at every size.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'The hexane-like pool has a mass transfer coefficient of 0.010163820387. What is it, and how does it reach the rate?', '["A rate in kg/s, which the pool area divides to give the evaporation flux of the pool.", "A dimensionless fraction of the pool that evaporates each second.", "A velocity in m/s; it multiplies Pv mu / (R T) to give the evaporation flux, which the pool area turns into the rate.", "The Schmidt number, which defaults to 0.8 when none is typed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 9: the model string is "km = 0.004786 u10^0.78 (2r)^-0.11 Sc^-0.67; q = km Pv mu / (R T) x A", and `massTransferCoefficientMS` carries m/s in its name. km times Pv mu / (R T) is the evaporation flux, 0.005749887804 kg/(m2 s), and times the area the rate. The Schmidt number is an input to km, defaulting to 0.8.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'What crosswind and vertical spreads does class D give at 300 m?', '["sigma_y 23.647903 m and sigma_z 14.948186 m.", "The same two figures, swapped.", "sigma_y 32.515866 m and sigma_z 23.310861 m, which are the two spreads the table prints for class C at 300 m.", "sigma_y 65.031732 m, sigma_z 60.000000 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sigma tables print 23.647903 m and 14.948186 m for class D at 300 m. Swapping 23.647903 and 14.948186 confuses the crosswind and vertical spreads. 32.515866 and 23.310861 m are class C, and 65.031732 and 60.000000 m are class A.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Class A''s sigma_z reads 20.000000 m at 100 m and 200.000000 m at 1000 m. Why exactly proportional to the distance?', '["Its sz2 and sz3 are both 0, so sigma_z = sz1 x = 0.2 x.", "All six classes are linear in the distance for sigma_z, since sigma_z is the vertical spread and the ground limits it.", "Class A is clamped to a linear form inside 100 m to 10 km, the advisory range that the curves are quoted for.", "Its sz1 is 0, so only the sz2 term survives."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The `BRIGGS_RURAL` table gives class A sz1 0.2, sz2 0 and sz3 0, so sigma_z = sz1 x (1 + sz2 x)^sz3 reduces to 0.2 x. Class D goes from 5.595029 m at 100 m to 37.947332 m at 1000 m, less than seven times as much, so the classes differ. Nothing is clamped, and sz1 is 0.2.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Closer in, only 200 m out, what does the neutral class give for UBIT on the axis at the ground?', '["4764.608684 mg/m3, the reading the engine gives at 100 m on the same centreline.", "1272.709839 mg/m3.", "1111.651553 mg/m3, the same point.", "239.712839 mg/m3."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The UBIT table prints 1272.709839 mg/m3 at 200 m in class D. 4764.608684 mg/m3 is 100 m. 1111.651553 is the same 200 m reading in ppm, a different unit. 239.712839 mg/m3 is 500 m; the sigmas at 200 m are 15.842361 and 10.524696 m, so the plume has spread.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'At 500 m the UBIT release gives 45.301469 mg/m3 in one class and 103.663813 mg/m3 in another. Which classes are they?', '["Class E and class F, the two stable classes, since the stable plume stays thin and near the ground.", "Class A and class B, since the two most unstable classes give the two lowest ground level readings at 500 m.", "Class C and class D, the two neutral classes in the middle of the table the engine exports.", "Class B and class C."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The class table at 500 m prints 45.301469 mg/m3 for B and 103.663813 mg/m3 for C. E and F give 555.698014 and 1562.900663 mg/m3. A gives 19.767914 mg/m3, the lowest. Only D is neutral, at 239.712839 mg/m3.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'An alarm reads 1200 ppm of carbon monoxide in a room at 293.15 K. As mass per cubic metre, what does the engine give?', '["1373.858385 mg/m3, the figure the conversion gives at 298.15 K.", "1499.600503 mg/m3, the figure the conversion gives at 273.15 K.", "1397.291071 mg/m3 at that temperature.", "1700.095669 mg/m3, the figure for hydrogen sulphide at the same 293.15 K."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Read the 1200 ppm carbon monoxide row in the 293.15 K column: 1397.291071. The neighbouring columns belong to warmer and colder air, and the conversion always uses the temperature stated. 1700.095669 comes from the hydrogen sulphide row, whose heavier molecule gives more mass for the same ppm.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'At the freezing point of water, 273.15 K, and one atmosphere, how many litres does a mole of ideal gas fill in the engine''s conversion?', '["22.413970 L/mol.", "24.45 L/mol, the CCOHS figure at every temperature.", "24.465404 L/mol, the molar volume the engine uses at 298.15 K.", "24.055117 L/mol, the molar volume the engine uses at 293.15 K."]'::jsonb, 0, 'R T / P at the freezing point gives 22.413970 L/mol, the smallest of the three temperatures tabled. Colder gas packs tighter, so the warmer rows carry the larger figures offered. The CCOHS number is a published approximation of the warmest row, and the engine always computes its own volume.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'In the plume expression, the term exp(-(z + h)^2 / 2 sz^2) sits beside exp(-(z - h)^2 / 2 sz^2). What does it stand for?', '["A correction for the stack''s own height, subtracted from the first term to account for the gas absorbed by the ground below the release.", "The crosswind spread of the plume, which the engine adds to the vertical term to widen the plume sideways.", "A second release of the same size above the first, used only for a stack.", "The image source at minus h: the ground reflects what reaches it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 11: the second exponential is the IMAGE SOURCE at minus h, and the ground reflects what reaches it. It is added to the first term, and total reflection absorbs nothing. The crosswind term is exp(-y^2 / 2 sy^2), a separate factor. The image sits below the ground at minus h for every release height, including a ground level one.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Under a clear night sky the UBIT gas drifts from the ground. How far downwind does the ground reading still exceed 100 mg/m3?', '["830.322126 m, the distance the table gives for the same target under class D.", "943.433416 m, which the table gives for class F falling to 500 mg/m3.", "9384.252298 m, the class F distance to 20 mg/m3.", "2564.337939 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A clear night with light wind is class F, and its 100 mg/m3 row reads 2564.337939 m. The neutral class stops far shorter. The other two class F figures answer the 500 and 20 mg/m3 targets, a higher and a lower threshold.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'How far downwind does the class D centreline stay above 500 mg/m3 for the UBIT release from the ground?', '["Out to 331.629944 m.", "Out to 943.433416 m, the distance to 500 mg/m3 that the table gives for class F.", "Out to 239.712839 m, since that is the concentration at 500 m.", "It never reaches 500 mg/m3, so the state is NOT_REACHED."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The table prints REACHED at 331.629944 m for class D and 500 mg/m3, and from the ground the concentration falls monotonically, so it is above 500 mg/m3 inside that distance. 943.433416 m is class F. 239.712839 is a concentration in mg/m3 and never a distance. The ground level centreline starts far above 500 mg/m3, 4764.608684 at 100 m.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'A much lower threshold, 20 mg/m3, is set for the elevated release. Which pair of distances bounds the ground zone above it?', '["At 2265.987260 m only.", "At 221.924194 m and 656.636313 m.", "Nowhere.", "At 150.672013 m on the way up and 2151.227430 m on the way down."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Twenty is far under the elevated plume''s maximum, so the curve crosses it once climbing and once falling: 150.672013 m and 2151.227430 m bound the zone. A single figure is what a ground release gives. The narrower pair answers the higher 100 mg/m3 threshold. Since the ground reading climbs past twenty, the threshold is certainly met.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'The elevated release''s table repeats one maximum, 153.887548 mg/m3 at 347.557177 m, on every target row. Why does the target never move it?', '["The maximum belongs to the plume itself, set by the release rate, the wind, the stack height and the class; a target only decides where that curve is crossed.", "The engine computes the maximum on the first row only and copies it to the other rows, which saves running a second search.", "It does move, but the six decimal print rounds away every change between the rows of the table.", "Every target in the table is REACHED."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 13 prints the same maximum beside targets of 500, 100, 20 and 5000 mg/m3: the ground curve under a 25 m stack is fixed before any target is named, and the target only picks the crossings, two for 100 and 20 and none for 500 and 5000. Nothing is copied between rows, rounding hides no change because there is none, and two of the four rows are NOT_REACHED.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Someone stands 20 m off the plume axis, half a kilometre downwind of UBIT in neutral weather. What reading do they get?', '["105.544135 mg/m3, 0.440294 of the centreline, the reading the crosswind table gives at 50 m.", "239.712839 mg/m3, since 20 m is well inside sigma_y.", "210.227800 mg/m3, a ratio of 0.876998 to the axis reading.", "9.008708 mg/m3, 0.037581 of the centreline, the reading the crosswind table gives at 100 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Twenty metres is small beside a sigma_y of 39.036003 m, so the Gaussian factor stays high at 0.876998 and the reading is 210.227800 mg/m3. The two lower figures belong to people standing farther out. Any offset at all lowers the reading, so the axis value itself is wrong too.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'A distance search returns a far distance of null. What can that mean?', '["The target was NOT_REACHED (the peak is below it) or the search ended BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE (still above it where the search stopped).", "The target is met at the source, which the plume cannot evaluate at a downwind distance of 0 m.", "The release is at ground level, where the far distance is always null and the near distance carries the answer.", "The engine refused the call."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 13: NOT_REACHED leaves both distances null, as the stack does at 500 mg/m3, and BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE returns a far distance of null, as class F to 20 mg/m3 capped at 2000 m does. From the ground the NEAR distance is the null one, and the far distance carries the answer. A refusal returns no distances at all.'),
  ('consequence', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'What does `plumeDistanceToConcentration` vary, and what does it hold fixed, while it searches?', '["It varies the crosswind distance at a fixed downwind distance until the reading equals the target.", "It varies the downwind distance and holds the receptor on the centreline at the receptor height.", "It varies the release height until the ground level reading equals the target.", "It varies the stability class from A to F at a fixed distance and reports the first class that meets the target."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 13: it finds the downwind distances at which the centreline concentration at the receptor height equals a target, by bisection. The crosswind offset is zero because the distance is always a centreline distance. The release height and the class are inputs to the search, and neither is searched over.');
